==English==
{{wp|carbuncle (disambiguation)}}
[[File:Escarboucle.png|right|thumb|A heraldic carbuncle.]]

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|carbuncle}}, {{m|enm|charbocle}}, from {{der|en|fro|carbuncle}}, {{m|fro|charbuncle}}, from {{der|en|la|carbunculus||a small coal; a reddish kind of precious stone; a kind of tumor}}, diminutive of {{m|la|carbō||a coal, charcoal}}. {{doublet|en|Garfinkel|Garfunkel}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkɑː.bʌŋ.kl̩/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkɑɹˌbʌŋ.kl̩/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vininn126-carbuncle.wav|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q12108659}} {{lb|en|archaic}} A deep-[[red]] or [[fiery]] colored [[garnet]] or other [[dark red]] [[precious stone]], especially when cut [[cabochon]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1602|author=w:William Shakespeare|title=w:Hamlet|section=act II, scene 2|line=401|passage=With eyes like '''carbuncles''', the hellish Pyrrhus {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Isaiah|54|12||And I will make thy windows of agates, and thy gates of '''carbuncles''', and all thy borders of pleasant stones.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1634|author=[[w:Sir Thomas Herbert, 1st Baronet|Thomas Herbert]]|title=A Relation of Some Yeares Trauaile, Begunne Anno 1626. Into Afrique and the Greater Asia, especially the Territories of the Persian Monarchie: And some Parts of the Orientall Indies, and Iles Adiacent. Of their Religion, Language, Habit, Discent, Ceremonies, and other Matters Concerning Them: Together with the Proceedings and Death of the Three Late Ambassadours: Sir D. C[otton] Sir R. S[herley] and the Persian Nogdi-Beg: As also the Two Great Monarchs, the King of Persia, and the Great Mogol|location=London|publisher={{w|William Stansby}} for Iacob Bloome|oclc=644078533|editor2=[[w:William Foster (historiographer)|William Foster]]|title2=Travels in Persia 1627–1629. Abridged and Edited by Sir William Foster {{upright|[...]}} with an Introduction and Notes {{upright|(Broadway Travellers)}}|location2=London|publisher2=[[w:Routledge|G. Routledge & Sons]]|year2=1928|oclc2=4900176|page2=79|passage=His turban, or mandil [''mandīl''], was of finest white silk interwoven with gold, bestudded with pearl[s] and '''carbuncles'''; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1936|author=Rollo Ahmed|title=The Black Art|publisher=Long|location=London|page=155|text=A piece of marigold or bay leaf was imbedded in the metal, and over it a '''carbuncle''' or chrysolite was placed.}}
## {{lb|en|heraldiccharge}} A [[charge]] or [[bearing]] supposed to represent the [[precious stone]], with eight [[sceptre]]s or [[stave#Noun|staves]] [[radiate|radiating]] from a common centre; an [[escarbuncle]].
# {{senseid|en|Q959922}} {{lb|en|pathology}} An [[abscess]] larger than a [[boil]], usually with one or more [[opening]]s [[drain#Verb|drain]]ing [[pus]] onto the [[skin]]. It is usually caused by [[staphylococcal]] [[infection]].
# An [[unpopular]] or [[ugly]] [[building]]; an [[eyesore]].

